  "transcript": "# NHS Accident and Emergency departments\n\n## How bad is the problem and why it matters\n> Long A&E waits and corridor care have risen sharply, increase patient harm and are unacceptable to leaders.\n\nChair: Since 2014 the 95% four-hour A&E target has not been hit. Long waits and corridor care have gone up, with serious harm—ONS analysis links 12+ hour waits to higher mortality.\nSamantha Jones: We share the committee’s alarm. Ministers are focused on ending long waits and corridor care; the Department will provide more detail on structural changes soon.\nDr Rowland: Every long wait is a person and family affected. Long waits—especially in A&E and corridors—cause harm and lengthen hospital stays, so our priority is eliminating them.\nSir Jim Mackey: We accept the NAO report. This is a whole-system problem: front door demand, hospital flow and discharge all connect. Winter and unexpected demand spikes make it harder.\n\n\n## Targets, beds and capacity\n> NHS leaders want to meet the 95% standard but give no firm date; more beds would help but funding and staffing are constraints.\n\nRupert Lowe: The constitution promises 95% within four hours. When will you achieve that?\nSir Jim Mackey: We aim for it daily but can’t commit a date from today; recovery will take longer than a couple of years given current demand and resources.\nRupert Lowe: NAO says 115k–120k beds needed vs ~103k now and you haven’t costed the gap; does that show a lack of seriousness?\nSir Jim Mackey: We know bed and ward costs but not an affordable national plan to open thousands more staffed beds. We’re also trying to reduce bed need by shifting care into same-day and community services.\nAnna Dixon: Professor Briggs, what’s a safe bed occupancy?\nProfessor Briggs: About 85% is reasonable; currently we’re higher and about 14.7% of bed days are for patients ready to go, so discharges are vital to free beds.\n\n\n## Corridor care: measuring it and tackling it\n> Corridor care is being measured more carefully and clinical-led interventions have halved breaches in some trusts, but reporting varies.\n\nChair: RCEM estimates 16,000 excess deaths linked to long waits. What will you do about corridor care?\nProfessor Briggs: We set a new definition (45 minutes in an undesignated space) and are measuring it; we’ve worked with trusts and halved corridor cases in targeted sites using GIRFT clinical leadership and whole-team change.\nRupert Lowe: How many were in corridors in September?\nProfessor Briggs: About 3,300 a day and roughly 99,000 a month—probably under-reported versus other figures.\nSamantha Jones: Ministers demand no tolerance for corridor care and have asked for a clear plan with timelines and responsibilities.\nMatt Turmaine: How do you spread good practice like West Herts that eradicated corridor care?\nDr Rowland: We visit high-performing sites, take model ED and model discharge practice, and run peer support; leadership and exec engagement are essential.\nProfessor Briggs: GIRFT provides clinical operational standards and teams to help trusts embed the changes—this winter we’re working intensively with 40 trusts.\n\n\n## Delayed discharge and social care\n> About a third of discharge delays relate to social care, and faster, joined-up discharge processes in hospitals could reclaim many lost bed days.\n\nAnna Dixon: How much of delayed discharge is social care and what are you doing about joined-up discharges?\nSamantha Jones: Roughly a third of delays are social-care related; DHSC and NHS England are working with local authorities through the better care fund and winter planning to unblock capacity.\nDr Rowland: A big proportion is pathway-zero process issues—paperwork, transport and ward-round timing. Fixing these hospital processes can free many bed days quickly.\nProfessor Briggs: We run pilots (RiO) to remove avoidable hospital-related delays and aim for lower occupancy over Christmas by improving discharge processes and weekend working.\nMr Betts: PCN projects that reduce frailty demand can save money but local ICBs say funding can’t continue—why is that?\nProfessor Briggs: PCN programmes show big reductions in ED referrals; we’ve asked ICBs to review the evidence—some will continue if they see the local case—and NHS England will follow up where funding decisions threaten proven pilots.\n\n\n## Demand, diagnostics and new care models\n> A&E demand has risen unexpectedly and patients are clinically more complex, driving up diagnostics and costs; same-day care and neighbourhood work reduce admissions.\n\nMr Betts: Why has A&E attendance risen? Is 111 to blame?\nSir Jim Mackey: We’ve seen about a 6% jump in urgent care attendance year-on-year; causes are unclear and fairly uniform nationally—better access to primary care, seasonal factors—no single cause identified.\nDr Rowland: Use of 111 rose over summer but referrals to A&E from 111 stayed steady; many people self-present believing they need help.\nMr Betts: You’ve increased staff in A&E yet per-patient costs rose and performance plateaued—why?\nDr Rowland: A&E cases are more complex (older, frail, comorbid) so diagnostics like CT/MRI are up ~50%; same-day care removes lower-complexity cases, leaving costlier case-mix in A&E.\nProfessor Briggs: Primary care networks that target frailty cut ED referrals by ~23% in visits I made; shifting care into neighbourhood services reduces demand on hospitals.\n\n\n## Data, accountability and preparing for winter\n> Leaders are improving realtime data and standards, holding boards to account, and preparing for winter, but data gaps, funding and staffing risks remain.\n\nAnna Dixon: How much does tech like Bradford’s control centre help?\nSir Jim Mackey: Live bed and flow data is crucial; many trusts lack the EPR and bed-management tech Bradford has. We’re pushing digital upgrades but they cost and take time.\nTristan Osborne: Are definitions of delays and corridor care consistent across trusts?\nDr Rowland: Corridor care has a new definition; reporting still varies. For discharge we now use pathway-based reports (P0–P3) to show needs and delays, though the data isn’t perfect.\nChair: Has industrial action affected progress?\nSir Jim Mackey: Strikes reduce headroom for improvement, cause short-term disruption and can leave longer-term recovery work unfinished; they complicate delivering change.\nSamantha Jones: For winter, DHSC and NHSE hold weekly senior review and expect ICBs and councils to sign board assurance; we’ve funded more urgent treatment centres and asked for clear plans on corridor care.\nProfessor Briggs: We’re also pushing seven-day discharge practices—keeping discharge lounges and ward rounds active at weekends to avoid Monday blockages.\nRupert Lowe: What enforcement do you have when trusts ignore guidance?\nSir Jim Mackey: We can intervene—replace boards, appoint turnaround directors—but intervention is a last resort and must be proportionate; sometimes national direction risks increasing local clinical risk if done without nuance.\nChair: You’ll publish an urgent and elective care strategy soon and the Committee will follow up. Thank you for the evidence this afternoon.\n",
